HTML: the structure of a page

Learn these tags first:

Page skeleton: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html>, <head>, <title>, <body>
Text: <h1>-<h6> (headings), <p> (paragraph), <span>, <strong>, <em>, <br>
Links and images: <a href="">, <img src="" alt="">
Lists: <ul>, <ol>, <li>
Containers: <div>, and the semantic ones <header>, <nav>, <main>, <section>, <footer>
Tables: <table>, <tr>, <th>, <td>
Forms: <form>, <input>, <label>, <button>, <select>, <textarea>
Forms in detail: input types (email, password, checkbox, radio), required, placeholder
Tables: colspan, rowspan
        Attribute	Joins	Direction
        colspan	    Columns	Left to right
        rowspan	    Rows	Top to bottom
Semantic HTML and accessibility: alt text, aria-label
    Tag	                     Purpose
    <header>	   Top of the page or section (logo, title)
    <nav>	       Group of navigation links
    <main>	       The main content (only one per page)
    <section>	   A group of related content, usually with a heading
    <article>      Content that makes sense on its own (blog post, news item)
    <aside>        Side content (related links, ads)
    <footer>	   Bottom of the page or section (copyright, contacts)
    
Media: <video>, <audio>, <iframe>

CSS: the look of a page

Learn these in order:

Selectors: tag (p), class (.card), id (#menu), and combinations (div p)
Text and colors: color, background-color, font-family, font-size
Box model: margin, padding, border, width, height. This is the most important concept.
Layout: Flexbox first (display: flex, justify-content, align-items), then Grid
Responsive design: media queries (@media (max-width: 600px))


