What is the box model?

In CSS, every HTML element is a rectangular box. The box has four layers, from inside to outside:

Content: the text or image itself
Padding: space between the content and the border (inside the box)
Border: the line around the padding
Margin: space between this box and other boxes (outside the box)

Think of a photo frame:

The photo is the content.
The white space between the photo and the frame is the padding.
The frame itself is the border.
The gap between this frame and the next frame on the wall is the margin.

width: 200px; sets the width of the content area only.
padding: 20px; adds 20px of space on all four sides, inside the border.
border: 5px solid navy; has three values: thickness, style (solid, dashed, dotted) and color.
margin: 30px; adds 30px of space outside the border, which pushes other boxes away.

.box {
  padding: 10px 20px;             /* top-bottom 10px, left-right 20px */
  margin: 10px 20px 30px 40px;    /* top, right, bottom, left (clockwise) */
  margin-left: 50px;              /* only one side */
  margin: 0 auto;                 /* centers a box that has a width */
}
